<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Allen Schmaltz
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Reexpress AI, Inc., USA
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">allen@re.express</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We solve the neural network robustness problem by adding <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Similarity</span> (i.e., correctly predicted depth-matches into training)-awareness and <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Distance</span>-to-training-distribution-awareness to the existing output <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Magnitude</span> (i.e., decision-boundary)-awareness of the softmax function. The resulting <span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_underline"><span id="abstract1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</span> activation function</span> provides strong signals of the relative epistemic (reducible) predictive uncertainty. We use this novel behavior to further address the complementary HCI problem of mapping the output to human-interpretable summary statistics over relevant partitions of a held-out calibration set. Estimates of prediction-conditional uncertainty are obtained via a parsimonious learned transform over the class-conditional empirical CDFs of the output of a final-layer <span id="abstract1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</span> activation function. For decision-making and as an intrinsic model check, estimates of class-conditional accuracy are obtained by further partitioning the high-probability regions of this calibrated output into class-conditional, region-specific CDFs. The uncertainty estimates from <span id="abstract1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_underline"><span id="abstract1.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</span> calibration</span> are remarkably robust to test-time distribution shifts and out-of-distribution inputs; incorporate awareness of the effective sample size; provide estimates of uncertainty from the learning and data splitting processes; and are well-suited for selective classification and conditional branching for additional test-time compute based on the predictive uncertainty, as for selective LLM generation, routing, and composition over multiple models and retrieval. Finally, we construct <span id="abstract1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_underline"><span id="abstract1.1.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</span> networks</span>, LLMs with uncertainty-aware verification and interpretability-by-exemplar as intrinsic properties. We provide open-source software implementing these results.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
          <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
          
          
          
        <a href="https://github.com/ReexpressAI/sdm" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/ReexpressAI/sdm</a></span></span></span></p>
  
</div>
<div id="id2" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_bottom">
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:4.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    <span class="ltx_rule ltx_align_center" style="width:100%;height:1.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) pose a challenge for interpretable and reliable deployment given the non-identifiability of their parameters <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, inter alia]</cite><span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
            
            
            
          Informally, this means that two or more distinct sets of values for the parameters can result in identical output distributions. As a consequence, interpreting the parameters of such models is typically much more complicated than with a simple linear regression model, for example.</span></span></span>, which can number in the billions or more. Instead of directly interpreting parameters, instance-based, metric-learner approximations and hard-attention mechanisms can be constructed with task-specific inductive biases for effective semi-supervised learning (i.e., feature detection) and introspection against the training set <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>, which can be useful for auditing predictions as a form of interpretability by example, or <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">exemplar</span>, over the representation space of the model. However, for real-world deployments, robust approaches for predictive uncertainty—and relatedly, for verifying the modeling process—are also needed, both for human decision-making and for constructing sequentially dependent LLM pipelines.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Known theoretical results limit the statistical quantities that can be derived over LLMs. Statistical assurances in the distribution-free setting are limited to approximately conditional quantities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, inter alia]</cite>. Further, even typical approximately conditional quantities can be difficult to obtain in practice, since the minimal assumption of exchangeability with a known held-out data set is itself often violated with co-variate and label shifts, which can be difficult to foresee with existing methods. Epistemologically, the prevalence of hallucinations and highly-confident wrong answers with widely deployed LLMs suggests a technical impasse in effectively modeling the predictive uncertainty, despite significant work from Bayesian, Frequentist, and empirically motivated perspectives <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, inter alia]</cite>. A foundational piece is evidently missing from the picture.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Given these intrinsic challenges, we approach the problem of uncertainty quantification over LLMs from a new angle and ask: <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Can we leverage the metric learning and dense matching capabilities of neural networks over high-dimensional inputs to at least aim to maximize, with minimal distributional assumptions, the separation of aleatoric (irreducible) uncertainty and epistemic (reducible) uncertainty, decomposing the sources of the latter in a manner that is interpretable and actionable?</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We answer this question in the affirmative with a conceptually parsimonious, LLM-driven partitioning of the data to decompose sources of epistemic uncertainty: Correctly predicted depth-matches into the training set (<math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Similarity" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Similarity</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Similarity</annotation></semantics></math>), the <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Distance" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Distance</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Distance</annotation></semantics></math> to the training set, and the distance to the decision-boundary (<math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Magnitude" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Magnitude</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Magnitude</annotation></semantics></math>). We use these signals to construct a new activation function, the <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation, which replaces a foundational building block of contemporary AI, the <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>softmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax</annotation></semantics></math> operation. A series of distributional transforms over an <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation then enable us to directly target a quantity of interest, <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">index-conditional calibration</span>, well-suited for selective classification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, inter alia]</cite>, which reflects the typical need for uncertainty quantification with LLMs as part of multi-stage decision pipelines. Finally, with this new foundational behavior, we construct a new LLM architecture, the <math id="S1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> network, with an intrinsic—and externally human interpretable—capability to verify its own instruction-following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, in this work:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce the <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation function, which encodes strong signals of epistemic uncertainty, to replace the <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>softmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax</annotation></semantics></math> operation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide a robust estimator of index-conditional uncertainty (Def. <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Definition 4.3. ‣ 4.2 From sdm Activation Functions to sdm Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>) via a final-layer <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation over existing models.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose the <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> network, a new LLM architecture and fine-tuning approach for which uncertainty-awareness and interpretability-by-exemplar are instrinsic properties.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We empirically compare the uncertainty-awareness of our estimators to existing classes of approaches, which we demonstrate do not reliably achieve our desired uncertainty quantity in the presence of—even modest—distribution shifts. As a natural, held-out blind evaluation, we also demonstrate efficiently uncovering undetected annotation errors in the carefully curated <span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">MMLU-Pro</span> benchmark dataset.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Motivation</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given the ability of LLM’s to recursively cross-encode data, user instructions, and outputs, if we had a reliable means of assessing the uncertainty over an LLM’s predictions that was also human interpretable (i.e., a quantifiable and verifiable assurance in their instruction-following abilities), such an LLM could serve as a <span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">universal verifier</span> over existing models, which would in effect calibrate the predictive uncertainty of other models: Have, e.g., an exogenous regression or multi-label model? Simply cross-encode the data, exogenous model, and output as input to the LLM verifier and let the neural network generate the accuracy as to whether the exogenous model is correct or not. This process could be repeated, as needed, using such an LLM as a basis for building complex, compound AI systems, recursively cross-encoding the input and output, using the uncertainty over discrete predictions as the branching condition for additional test-time compute, tool calling, and human feedback — and ultimately, reliable AI-assisted decision-making. In this work, we introduce the mechanisms for constructing such a verifier, which we formalize below.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Setting</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Both LLM next-token prediction and standard classification tasks (e.g., predicting the sentiment of a movie review) are formulated similarly as predictions over discrete classes.
We are given a training dataset, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit=\{({\bm{x}}_{n},y_{n})\}_{n=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit=\{({\bm{x}}_{n},y_{n})\}_{n=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> of inputs, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}\in{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}\in{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>, paired with their corresponding ground-truth discrete labels, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in{\mathcal{Y}}=\{1,\ldots,C\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in{\mathcal{Y}}=\{1,\ldots,C\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a labeled calibration dataset, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math>, drawn from the same distribution as <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math>. We are then given a new test instance, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>, from an unlabeled test set, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\testSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>te</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\testSplit</annotation></semantics></math>, and seek to estimate the label with a prediction, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math>, via the un-normalized log probabilities (“logits”, informally) of a final linear layer: <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}={\bm{W}}^{T}{\bm{h}}+{\bm{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒉</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝒃</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}={\bm{W}}^{T}{\bm{h}}+{\bm{b}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{h}}=\underlyingNetwork({\bm{x}};\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>network</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{h}}=\underlyingNetwork({\bm{x}};\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> is the final hidden state of a network parameterized by <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>. The network can be recurrent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, convolutional <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, or self-attention-based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, among others. The discrete prediction is taken as <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}=\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}{{\bm{z}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mi>𝒛</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}=\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}{{\bm{z}}}</annotation></semantics></math>; however, for learning <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑾</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒃</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{b}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and for human decision-making, we also seek an estimate of the predictive uncertainty, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p({\bm{y}}\,|\,{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p({\bm{y}}\,|\,{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is typically obtained by normalizing <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒛</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}</annotation></semantics></math> via the <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>softmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax</annotation></semantics></math> operation described next. We will make a distinction between models, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> (defined by <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑾</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒃</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{b}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and when applicable, the exemplar adaptor, described below), which produce the prediction, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math>, and estimators, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which provide an estimate of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p({\bm{y}}\,|\,{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p({\bm{y}}\,|\,{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>, because different estimators can be used over the same model.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Softmax and the Cross-Entropy loss</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>softmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax</annotation></semantics></math> has as its origins the work of L. Boltzmann in the 19th century <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[see <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, inter alia]</cite>. It remains a central function in the natural and engineering sciences. It is ubiquitous in deep learning, playing an integral role as a router in self-attention mechanisms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite> and mixture-of-experts models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>; forming the basis of the cross-entropy loss used for next-token training of LLMs; and serving as the final interface between a model and the end-user, converting the un-normalized model logits to human interpretable probability distributions, at least in principle:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\softmax({\bm{z}})_{i}=\frac{e^{\tau\cdot z_{i}}}{\sum^{C}_{c=1}{e^{\tau\cdot z_{c}}}},1\leq i\leq C,\tau\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\softmax({\bm{z}})_{i}=\frac{e^{\tau\cdot z_{i}}}{\sum^{C}_{c=1}{e^{\tau\cdot z_{c}}}},1\leq i\leq C,\tau\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The above function induces a parameterization of the event probabilities of a categorical distribution:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\CategoricalDistribution(C=|{\mathcal{Y}}|,\softmax({\bm{z}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Categorical</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\CategoricalDistribution(C=|{\mathcal{Y}}|,\softmax({\bm{z}}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The inverse-temperature parameter, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, controls the sharpness of the distribution. As <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\rightarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>, the output of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax({\bm{z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax({\bm{z}})</annotation></semantics></math> converges to a uniform distribution where each class has probability <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>C</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{C}</annotation></semantics></math>; as <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\rightarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\rightarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, the output converges to a distribution in which all of the mass is assigned to a single class. In deep learning, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is treated as a learnable, <span id="S3.SS2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">global</span> hyper-parameter; <span id="S3.SS2.p1.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">instance-wise</span> variation in the distance to the decision-boundary is thus determined by the relative <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Magnitude" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Magnitude</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Magnitude</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{\hat{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{\hat{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This model is learned by minimizing the cross-entropy loss between <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒛</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the index of the true labels over <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math>. The <span id="S3.SS2.p1.3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">natural</span> logarithm of the loss is the counterpart to the base <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> of the <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>softmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathcal{L}}(\theta,{\bm{W}},{\bm{b}};\trainSplit)=-\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n}^{N}\log_{e}\left(\frac{e^{\tau\cdot z_{y_{n}}}}{\sum^{C}_{c=1}{e^{\tau\cdot z_{c}}}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub></mrow></msup><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathcal{L}}(\theta,{\bm{W}},{\bm{b}};\trainSplit)=-\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n}^{N}\log_{e}\left(\frac{e^{\tau\cdot z_{y_{n}}}}{\sum^{C}_{c=1}{e^{\tau\cdot z_{c}}}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Methods</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we revisit Eq. <a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.2 Softmax and the Cross-Entropy loss ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, <a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3.2 Softmax and the Cross-Entropy loss ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, and  <a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.2 Softmax and the Cross-Entropy loss ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> given new observations on the statistical behavior of high-dimensional objects, empirically derived from large parameter neural networks. We will seek to decouple the sources of epistemic uncertainty via a new activation function that is conceptually:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sdm({\bm{z}})_{i}=\frac{{\Similarity}^{\Distance\cdot\Magnitude_{i}}}{\sum^{C}_{c=1}{{\Similarity}^{\Distance\cdot\Magnitude_{c}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Similarity</mtext><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Distance</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Magnitude</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></msubsup><msup><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Similarity</mtext><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Distance</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Magnitude</mtext><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sdm({\bm{z}})_{i}=\frac{{\Similarity}^{\Distance\cdot\Magnitude_{i}}}{\sum^{C}_{c=1}{{\Similarity}^{\Distance\cdot\Magnitude_{c}}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with a corresponding negative log likelihood loss that takes into account the change of base (§ <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 From Model Approximations via Exemplar Adaptors to sdm Activation Functions ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>). We will additionally introduce a transformation that rescales this value for an <span id="S4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">instance</span> with exogenous information <span id="S4.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">across</span> <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math>, effectively calibrating <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> the model to produce reliable, interpretable probabilities (§ <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 From sdm Activation Functions to sdm Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>). Finally, we integrate this behavior into the LLM architecture and training, yielding an LLM with an intrinsic ability to verify its own instruction following (§ <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 From sdm Calibration to sdm Networks ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>From Model Approximations via Exemplar Adaptors to <span id="S4.SS1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</span> Activation Functions</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Exemplar adaptors, 1-D CNN adaptors (with a final linear layer) over the frozen hidden states of a network, induce distilled, compressed representations of an underlying network’s representation space conditional on its predictions. This behavior can be used to faithfully approximate a model’s predictions as a mapping against a training, or support, set. This can be achieved, for example, with instance-based, metric-learning estimators, such as weighted KNNs, where the weights are learned as a transform of the exemplar adaptor’s distilled representations.<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
            Such instance-based, metric-learner approximations of neural networks differ from traditional KNN rules <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, inter alia]</cite> in two critical respects: The neural network serves as a semi-supervised learner of the distances between the dense representations that identify the instances, and there is a model prediction (in addition to the ground-truth label) for each instance in the support set. The former enables effective partitioning despite the curse of high dimensions; the latter provides an additional indicator of reliability for each instance.
</span></span></span> Critically, when the approximations diverge from the predictions of the underlying model, the inputs tend to be from the subsets of the distribution over which the underlying model is itself unreliable <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>. In other words, the approximations encode strong signals of the epistemic uncertainty, a point under-appreciated in the existing literature and which we bring to its logical conclusion in this work. Rather than constructing explicit KNN approximations, which require a separate training step and additional parameters, we instead <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">q</span>uantize the closeness of a point to the training set with a discrete estimate. Further, we transform the distance to the closest match as a quantile estimate over the distribution of distances. These quantities, combined with the output <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Magnitude" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Magnitude</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Magnitude</annotation></semantics></math>, capture the key sources of epistemic uncertainty for an input <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">instance</span> (cf. § <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 From sdm Activation Functions to sdm Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.1 </span>Exemplar Adaptor</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We take as the CNN of our exemplar adaptor <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:({\bm{h}},t({\bm{z}}))\in\mathbb{R}^{D}\mapsto{\bm{h}}^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msup><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>M</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:({\bm{h}},t({\bm{z}}))\in\mathbb{R}^{D}\mapsto{\bm{h}}^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, a 1-D CNN that takes as input <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> (if available) of the underlying network and optionally, the concatenation of the output of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t({\bm{z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t({\bm{z}})</annotation></semantics></math>, a transform of the underlying network’s output.<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
                
                
                
              For black-box LLM API’s in particular, we will not have direct access to <math id="footnote4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> and will instead construct a proxy of <math id="footnote4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> via a transform <math id="footnote4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> of the available output, which may (and typically will with current models) itself be the result of a <math id="footnote4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>softmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax</annotation></semantics></math> operation.
</span></span></span>
The CNN has <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> filters, the filter applications of which produce <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{h}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{h}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, the distilled representation of the underlying network. A final linear layer, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}^{\prime}={\bm{W}}^{\prime T}{\bm{h}}^{\prime}+{\bm{b}}^{\prime},{\bm{z}}^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>C</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}^{\prime}={\bm{W}}^{\prime T}{\bm{h}}^{\prime}+{\bm{b}}^{\prime},{\bm{z}}^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, then replaces the underlying network’s linear layer, with the discrete prediction taken as <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}=\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}{{\bm{z}}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}=\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}{{\bm{z}}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This exemplar adaptor will then enable us to derive the key signals of epistemic uncertainty, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Similarity" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Similarity</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Similarity</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Distance" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Distance</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Distance</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Magnitude" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Magnitude</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Magnitude</annotation></semantics></math> described next.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.2 </span><math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Similarity" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Similarity</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Similarity</annotation></semantics></math></h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We define the <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Similarity" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Similarity</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Similarity</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\q</annotation></semantics></math>) of an instance to the training set as the count of consecutive nearest matches in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math> that are correctly predicted <span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and</span> match <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math> of the new, unseen instance. Concretely, we first sort <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{D}}_{\rm{tr}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{D}}_{\rm{tr}}</annotation></semantics></math> (for which we have both model predictions and ground-truth labels) based on the <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> distance (2-norm) from <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{h}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{h}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left[({\bm{x}}^{tr}_{(1)},\hat{y}^{tr}_{(1)},y^{tr}_{(1)}),\ldots,({\bm{x}}^{tr}_{(N)},\hat{y}^{tr}_{(N)},y^{tr}_{(N)})\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left[({\bm{x}}^{tr}_{(1)},\hat{y}^{tr}_{(1)},y^{tr}_{(1)}),\ldots,({\bm{x}}^{tr}_{(N)},\hat{y}^{tr}_{(N)},y^{tr}_{(N)})\right]</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="||{\bm{h}}^{\prime}-{\bm{h}}^{\prime tr}_{(1)}||_{2}\leq\ldots\leq||{\bm{h}}^{\prime}-{\bm{h}}^{\prime tr}_{(N)}||_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒉</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒉</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">||{\bm{h}}^{\prime}-{\bm{h}}^{\prime tr}_{(1)}||_{2}\leq\ldots\leq||{\bm{h}}^{\prime}-{\bm{h}}^{\prime tr}_{(N)}||_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then calculate <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in\{0,\ldots,|\trainSplit|\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in\{0,\ldots,|\trainSplit|\}</annotation></semantics></math> as:</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=\sum^{|\trainSplit|}_{i=1}\mathbf{1}_{\hat{y}=\hat{y}^{\rm{tr}}_{(i)}}\cdot\mathbf{1}_{\hat{y}^{\rm{tr}}_{(i)}=y^{\rm{tr}}_{(i)}}\cdot\mathbf{1}_{i-1=\sum^{i-1}_{j=1}\mathbf{1}_{\hat{y}=\hat{y}^{\rm{tr}}_{(j)}}\cdot\mathbf{1}_{\hat{y}^{\rm{tr}}_{(j)}=y^{\rm{tr}}_{(j)}}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi mathsize="0.980em">tr</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>tr</mi></msubsup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>tr</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>tr</mi></msubsup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mstyle><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>tr</mi></msubsup></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>tr</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>tr</mi></msubsup></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=\sum^{|\trainSplit|}_{i=1}\mathbf{1}_{\hat{y}=\hat{y}^{\rm{tr}}_{(i)}}\cdot\mathbf{1}_{\hat{y}^{\rm{tr}}_{(i)}=y^{\rm{tr}}_{(i)}}\cdot\mathbf{1}_{i-1=\sum^{i-1}_{j=1}\mathbf{1}_{\hat{y}=\hat{y}^{\rm{tr}}_{(j)}}\cdot\mathbf{1}_{\hat{y}^{\rm{tr}}_{(j)}=y^{\rm{tr}}_{(j)}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the rightmost indicator function, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, ensures consecutive (depth-wise) matches. By definition, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\q</annotation></semantics></math> cannot exceed the count of the most prevalent class label in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math>, and since we assume a reasonable relative number of points for each class, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\q\ll|\trainSplit|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\q\ll|\trainSplit|</annotation></semantics></math> is typical. For the special case of calculating <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}\in\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}\in\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math>, which only occurs during learning, we exclude the self-match.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.3 </span><math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Distance" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Distance</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Distance</annotation></semantics></math></h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> distance to the nearest match in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math> follows from above: <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dNearest=||{\bm{h}}^{\prime}-{\bm{h}}^{\prime tr}_{(1)}||_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>nearest</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mi>tr</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dNearest=||{\bm{h}}^{\prime}-{\bm{h}}^{\prime tr}_{(1)}||_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, it is difficult to work with <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dNearest" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>nearest</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dNearest</annotation></semantics></math> directly since its scale can vary widely depending on the input to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and the size of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. Instead, we define <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Distance" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Distance</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Distance</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dVal\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dVal\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, in terms of the class-wise empirical CDFs of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dNearest" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>nearest</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dNearest</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math>, as the most conservative quantile relative to the distance to the nearest matches observed in the labeled, held-out set:</p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dVal=\min\left[1-{\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{1}}_{\rm{ca}}(\dNearest),\ldots,1-{\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{C}}_{\rm{ca}}(\dNearest)\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>eCDF</mi><mi>ca</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>nearest</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>eCDF</mi><mi>ca</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>nearest</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dVal=\min\left[1-{\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{1}}_{\rm{ca}}(\dNearest),\ldots,1-{\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{C}}_{\rm{ca}}(\dNearest)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The empirical CDFs are determined by the labeled points in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math> for which <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\q&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\q&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, where, as indicated by the superscripts, the stratification of points is by the true labels, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>. For example, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{1}}_{\rm{ca}}(\dNearest)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>eCDF</mi><mi>ca</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>nearest</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{1}}_{\rm{ca}}(\dNearest)</annotation></semantics></math> is the empirical CDF of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dNearest" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>nearest</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dNearest</annotation></semantics></math> values in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math> for which <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=1</annotation></semantics></math>, a notation convention we will use throughout. (Points with <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\q=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\q=0</annotation></semantics></math> are effectively out-of-distribution points and treated as such in downstream decision-making, so they are excluded to avoid biasing the estimates.) At test-time, we do not see <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>; instead, the minimum is calculated over the quantiles of each of the class-conditional eCDFs, regardless of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math>. As with <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, for the special case of calculating <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dVal" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dVal</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}\in\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}\in\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math>, we replace <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{c}}_{\rm{ca}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>eCDF</mi><mi>ca</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{c}}_{\rm{ca}}</annotation></semantics></math> with the analogous <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{c}}_{\rm{tr}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>eCDF</mi><mi>tr</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{eCDF}}^{y_{c}}_{\rm{tr}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the class-wise empirical CDFs of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dNearest" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>nearest</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dNearest</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math> excluding self-matches.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.4 </span><math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Magnitude" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Magnitude</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Magnitude</annotation></semantics></math></h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We take as the <math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Magnitude" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Magnitude</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Magnitude</annotation></semantics></math>, or distance to the decision boundary, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{\prime}_{\hat{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{\prime}_{\hat{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>, as in the standard <math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>softmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax</annotation></semantics></math> case but via <math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> from the linear layer of the exemplar adaptor.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.5 </span><math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> Activation: Formulation</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the above quantities to define the <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation function:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime})_{i}=\frac{(2+q)^{\dVal\cdot z^{\prime}_{i}}}{\sum^{C}_{c=1}{(2+q)^{\dVal\cdot z^{\prime}_{c}}}},1\leq i\leq C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></msup><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime})_{i}=\frac{(2+q)^{\dVal\cdot z^{\prime}_{i}}}{\sum^{C}_{c=1}{(2+q)^{\dVal\cdot z^{\prime}_{c}}}},1\leq i\leq C</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The output distribution becomes sharper with higher values of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\q</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dVal" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dVal</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Also note that when <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dNearest" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>nearest</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dNearest</annotation></semantics></math> exceeds the largest distance observed in the labeled data, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dVal=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dVal=0</annotation></semantics></math> and the output distribution is uniform, reflecting a maximally high (i.e., out-of-distribution) epistemic uncertainty estimate. The standard <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>softmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=1</annotation></semantics></math> is recovered by setting <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=e-2,d=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=e-2,d=1</annotation></semantics></math>. As with the <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>softmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax</annotation></semantics></math> operation, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime})=\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}{\bm{z}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}\sdm({\bm{z}}^{\prime})=\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}{\bm{z}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS6" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.6 </span><math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> Activation: Loss and Training</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A loss analogous to Eq. <a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.2 Softmax and the Cross-Entropy loss ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> then follows with the applicable change of base. We use this loss to train the weights of the exemplar adaptor, which includes the parameters of the linear layer (<math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{b}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{b}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>), as well as the convolution weights and biases, which we collectively represent with <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{G}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The weights of the underlying <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underlyingNetwork" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>network</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underlyingNetwork</annotation></semantics></math> remain fixed. (We return to training <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑾</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒃</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{b}}</annotation></semantics></math> of an underlying LLM in § <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 From sdm Calibration to sdm Networks ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>.)</p>
<table id="S4.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathcal{L}}({\bm{G}},{\bm{W}}^{\prime},{\bm{b}}^{\prime};\trainSplit)=-\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n}^{N}\log_{(2+q)}\left(\frac{(2+q)^{\dVal\cdot z^{\prime}_{y_{n}}}}{\sum^{C}_{c=1}{(2+q)^{\dVal\cdot z^{\prime}_{c}}}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑮</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></msup><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathcal{L}}({\bm{G}},{\bm{W}}^{\prime},{\bm{b}}^{\prime};\trainSplit)=-\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n}^{N}\log_{(2+q)}\left(\frac{(2+q)^{\dVal\cdot z^{\prime}_{y_{n}}}}{\sum^{C}_{c=1}{(2+q)^{\dVal\cdot z^{\prime}_{c}}}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Pseudo-code for training the <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation layer and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> estimator (described in § <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 From sdm Activation Functions to sdm Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, next) appears in Alg. <a href="#alg1" title='Algorithm 1 ‣ 4.1.6 "sdm" Activation: Loss and Training ‣ 4.1 From Model Approximations via Exemplar Adaptors to sdm Activation Functions ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. The first epoch is initialized with a standard <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>softmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., setting <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=e-2,d=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=e-2,d=1</annotation></semantics></math>). Training then proceeds by re-estimating <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}\in\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}\in\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math> after each epoch. We take as the stopping criteria for one learning round as the epoch with the highest average balanced (across classes) <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> values over <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math>. We choose the final model <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{*}\in{\mathbb{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝕄</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{*}\in{\mathbb{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> iterations of random shuffles and splits of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math> and parameter initializations as that with the globally highest average balanced (across classes) <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> values over <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math>. For learning, we assume <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math> are balanced across all class labels, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> <math id="alg1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> Activation Layer and <math id="alg1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> Estimator Training</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1:</span>
                
                
              <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underlyingNetwork" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>network</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underlyingNetwork</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="alg1.l1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">max epochs</span>, <span id="alg1.l1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">rescaler max epochs</span>, <span id="alg1.l1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">rescaler stopping condition</span> 
</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2:</span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption:</span> <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math> are balanced across all class labels, <math id="alg1.l2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">3:</span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">procedure</span> <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm-iterative-train</span>(<math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underlyingNetwork" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>network</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underlyingNetwork</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="alg1.l3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">max epochs</span>)

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">4:</span>
                
                
                <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{*}\leftarrow\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{*}\leftarrow\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l4.2" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> Globally best model
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">5:</span>
                
                
                <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit_{*}\leftarrow\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit_{*}\leftarrow\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit_{*}\leftarrow\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit_{*}\leftarrow\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l5.2" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> Data splits of best model
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">6:</span>
                
                
                <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}\leftarrow\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}\leftarrow\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l6.2" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="alg1.l6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> estimator (i.e., <math id="alg1.l6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pLower" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>lower</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pLower</annotation></semantics></math>)
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">7:</span>
                
                
                <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{metric}}_{*}\leftarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>metric</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{metric}}_{*}\leftarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l7.2" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> Determines final best model
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">8:</span>
                
                
                <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{stats}}\leftarrow\{~\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>stats</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.330em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{stats}}\leftarrow\{~\}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l8.2" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> Summary statistics to calculate <math id="alg1.l8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\CauchyMinValidQBin,\operatorname{\rm{m}}^{\hat{y}}_{\text{$\left\lfloor\tilde{q}\right\rfloor$}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>min</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\CauchyMinValidQBin,\operatorname{\rm{m}}^{\hat{y}}_{\text{$\left\lfloor\tilde{q}\right\rfloor$}}</annotation></semantics></math> (§ <a href="#S4.SS2.SSS4" title="4.2.4 Accounting for Uncertainty in the Data Splitting and Learning Processes ‣ 4.2 From sdm Activation Functions to sdm Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2.4</span></a>)
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">9:</span>
                
                
                <span id="alg1.l9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in 1,\ldots,J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in 1,\ldots,J</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span> <span id="alg1.l9.4" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> The learning process is repeated <math id="alg1.l9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> times
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">10:</span>
                
                
                 <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{j*}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{j*}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l10.2" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> Best model for a single learning round
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">11:</span>
                
                
                 <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rm{metric}_{j}\leftarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>metric</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rm{metric}_{j}\leftarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">12:</span>
                
                
                 <math id="alg1.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> Random shuffle and even split of <math id="alg1.l12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="alg1.l12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">13:</span>
                
                
                 <math id="alg1.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{j}\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{j}\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> Random initialization of <math id="alg1.l13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{G}}_{j},{\bm{W}}^{\prime}_{j},{\bm{b}}^{\prime}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑮</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{G}}_{j},{\bm{W}}^{\prime}_{j},{\bm{b}}^{\prime}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">14:</span>
                
                
                 <math id="alg1.l14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\q\leftarrow e-2,d\leftarrow 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\q\leftarrow e-2,d\leftarrow 1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l14.2" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> Standard <math id="alg1.l14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>softmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax</annotation></semantics></math> for first epoch
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">15:</span>
                
                
                 <span id="alg1.l15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in 1,\ldots,\text{max epochs}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext>max epochs</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in 1,\ldots,\text{max epochs}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l16" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">16:</span>
                
                
                   Minimize <math id="alg1.l16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}({\bm{G}},{\bm{W}}^{\prime},{\bm{b}}^{\prime};\trainSplit)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑮</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}({\bm{G}},{\bm{W}}^{\prime},{\bm{b}}^{\prime};\trainSplit)</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l16.2" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> Eq. <a href="#S4.E8" title='In 4.1.6 "sdm" Activation: Loss and Training ‣ 4.1 From Model Approximations via Exemplar Adaptors to sdm Activation Functions ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>
</span>
</div>
