</div>
<div id="alg4.l20" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">20:</span>
                  
                  
                  <span id="alg4.l20.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg4.l20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{*},\trainSplit_{*},\calibrationSplit_{*},\sdmVerificationLayer_{*},{\mathcal{E}}_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mmultiscripts><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo>,</mo><mmultiscripts><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo>,</mo><msub><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">verificationLayer</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{*},\trainSplit_{*},\calibrationSplit_{*},\sdmVerificationLayer_{*},{\mathcal{E}}_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg4.l21" class="ltx_listingline">
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">21:</span>
                  
                  
                <math id="alg4.l21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{*},\trainSplit_{*},\calibrationSplit_{*},\sdmVerificationLayer_{*},{\mathcal{E}}_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mmultiscripts><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo>,</mo><mmultiscripts><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo>,</mo><msub><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">verificationLayer</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{*},\trainSplit_{*},\calibrationSplit_{*},\sdmVerificationLayer_{*},{\mathcal{E}}_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph"><math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> Network Test-time Generation.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At test-time, we generate from <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm({\bm{z}}_{\rm{neg}},{\bm{z}}_{\rm{pos}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>neg</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>pos</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm({\bm{z}}_{\rm{neg}},{\bm{z}}_{\rm{pos}})</annotation></semantics></math> up to the output control token, or end-of-sequence token, at the unit-of-analysis of the verification labels, via greedy (i.e., <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}</annotation></semantics></math>) decoding with <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\q=e-2,d=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\q=e-2,d=1</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., equivalent to <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>softmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax</annotation></semantics></math>).<span id="footnote17" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">17</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">17</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">17</span>
                  
                  
                  
                As previously noted, at test-time, <math id="footnote17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\left(\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}(\sdm({\bm{z}}_{\rm{neg}},{\bm{z}}_{\rm{pos}}))~{\rm{mod}}~|{\mathcal{V}}|\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>neg</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>pos</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.330em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>mod</mi><mo fence="false" lspace="0.330em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}(\sdm({\bm{z}}_{\rm{neg}},{\bm{z}}_{\rm{pos}}))~{\rm{mod}}~|{\mathcal{V}}|\right)</annotation></semantics></math> maps back to the original token symbol in the vocabulary when decoding over the joint distribution.</span></span></span> We then continue generation, or take other branching actions, based on <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pLower" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>lower</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pLower</annotation></semantics></math> from the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdmVerificationLayer" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">verificationLayer</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdmVerificationLayer</annotation></semantics></math>, which by extension, also provides interpretability-by-exemplar into <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math> via matching (from <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\q</annotation></semantics></math>) and against similarly calibrated points in <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math> via <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hardqbin" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hardqbin</annotation></semantics></math>. Each classification via the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.Px4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdmVerificationLayer" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">verificationLayer</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdmVerificationLayer</annotation></semantics></math> requires on the order of the computation needed for commonly used dense retrieval augmentations of LLMs, so such test-time generation and verification is achievable even using edge devices.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We comprehensively evaluate the uncertainty-awareness of our estimators across a representative set of the existing classes of estimators over LLMs. First, we compare <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> calibration to existing approaches in a standard classification setting, using open-source models at a scale that can be readily replicated with consumer-level compute (§ <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Experiments: Classification ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>). Next, we show how an <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> estimator can be applied to a fully black-box LLM API, only with access to the top output logits and without a proxy model running in parallel, using the standard <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetMMLU" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">MMLU</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetMMLU</annotation></semantics></math> benchmark (§ <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Experiments: Black-box LLM APIs ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>). In this context, we also consider a data quality experiment in which we seek to detect errors in the carefully curated <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">MMLU-Pro</span> dataset. This serves as a natural, held-out blind evaluation. Finally, we examine the universal verification behavior of a <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> network by training over a composition of the classification tasks examined in the first set of targeted experiments (§ <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3 Experiments: Verified Generation ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Experiments: Classification</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Before introducing the additional complications of LLM generation, we first isolate the core calibration behavior against existing classes of approaches in standard multi-class classification settings.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.1 </span>Task: <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetSentiment" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Sentiment</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetSentiment</annotation></semantics></math></h4>

<section id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Task.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our first task (<math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetSentiment" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Sentiment</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetSentiment</annotation></semantics></math>) is predicting the sentiment of movie reviews using the commonly used benchmark data of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>. This is a binary classification task with <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\{0={\rm{negative}},1={\rm{positive}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>negative</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>positive</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\{0={\rm{negative}},1={\rm{positive}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math> are constructed from a total of 18k instances. The held-out set for evaluation, <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\testSplit|=1583" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>te</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1583</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\testSplit|=1583</annotation></semantics></math>, is from the same distribution as <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math>. This is a well-studied task for which the surface-level signals correlated with the target labels are expected to be effectively modeled by large parameter LLMs; as such, relatively high task accuracies are expected.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Models.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our base <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underlyingNetwork" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>network</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underlyingNetwork</annotation></semantics></math>, the parameters of which stay fixed and are used for all estimators, is the open-source, publicly available <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Faster I</span> model from the on-device data analysis program <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Reexpress one<span id="footnote18" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">18</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">18</sup>
                    <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote18.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">18</span></span>
                    
                    
                    
                  <a href="https://github.com/ReexpressAI/Reexpress_one" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_upright">https://github.com/ReexpressAI/Reexpress_one</a></span></span></span></span> from Reexpress AI. This 1.2 billion-parameter model is a late fusion of the encoder and decoder of Flan-T5 large <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> and mT0-base <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>. We discard the existing adaptor layers that are part of the on-device program and only use the parameter fusion of the encoder and decoder, adding the adaptors and estimators introduced in this work. We take the mean of the hidden states across input tokens, resulting in a hidden state of <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{h}}\in\mathbb{R}^{3774}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3774</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{h}}\in\mathbb{R}^{3774}</annotation></semantics></math> as input to the exemplar adaptors and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation layers with <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=1000</annotation></semantics></math>. We use the label <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\modelFasterICNNAdaptor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">FasterI+adaptor</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\modelFasterICNNAdaptor</annotation></semantics></math> for a standard exemplar adaptor over <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{h}}\in\mathbb{R}^{3774}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3774</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{h}}\in\mathbb{R}^{3774}</annotation></semantics></math> trained with a cross-entropy loss, and the label <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\modelFasterISDM" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">FasterI+sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\modelFasterISDM</annotation></semantics></math> for the <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation layer over <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{h}}\in\mathbb{R}^{3774}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3774</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{h}}\in\mathbb{R}^{3774}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Estimators.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Holding <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underlyingNetwork" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>network</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underlyingNetwork</annotation></semantics></math> constant, we examine representative classes of estimators used with neural networks, seeking index-conditional calibration at <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\prime}=0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.95</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\prime}=0.95</annotation></semantics></math>. At the most basic, but also, perhaps the most commonly used in practice, representing the absence of a post-hoc calibration method, we simply threshold the output, <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax({\bm{z}})\geq\alpha^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax({\bm{z}})\geq\alpha^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the temperature <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=1</annotation></semantics></math>. As an established empirical approach for calibrating neural networks, we provide a comparison to temperature scaling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, a single parameter version of post-hoc Platt-scaling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, with the label <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\estimatorTempScaling" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">tempScaling</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\estimatorTempScaling</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, the estimator is the thresholding of the output <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax({\bm{z}};\tau)\geq\alpha^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax({\bm{z}};\tau)\geq\alpha^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> after learning a value for <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math>. We also provide a comparison to two representative conformal predictors, the <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\conformalAPS" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">APS</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\conformalAPS</annotation></semantics></math> method of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Romano et al. [35]</a></cite> and the adaptiveness-optimized <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\conformalRAPS" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">RAPS</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\conformalRAPS</annotation></semantics></math> algorithm of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Angelopoulos et al. [2]</a></cite>. The admission criteria for the <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\conformalAPS" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">APS</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\conformalAPS</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\conformalRAPS" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">RAPS</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\conformalRAPS</annotation></semantics></math> estimators is prediction sets of size 1, using an <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.05</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We then compare to the primary <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> estimator <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pLower" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>lower</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pLower</annotation></semantics></math>, as well as the reference comparisons <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pCentroid" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>centroid</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pCentroid</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pUpper" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>upper</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pUpper</annotation></semantics></math>, as defined in § <a href="#S4.SS2.SSS5" title="4.2.5 Index-Conditional Calibration ‣ 4.2 From sdm Activation Functions to sdm Calibration ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2.5</span></a>. We train the <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation layer and estimator (Alg. <a href="#alg1" title='Algorithm 1 ‣ 4.1.6 "sdm" Activation: Loss and Training ‣ 4.1 From Model Approximations via Exemplar Adaptors to sdm Activation Functions ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) with <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J=10</annotation></semantics></math>, here and for the remaining experiments. Additional training hyper-parameters and details shared across all experiments are provided in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 Additional Training Details ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As a common point of reference, here and for all other experiments as well, we will use the label <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\estimatorNoReject" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">no-reject</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\estimatorNoReject</annotation></semantics></math> to refer to the model predictions without any selective filtering (i.e., the raw output accuracies, either from a <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmax" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>softmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmax</annotation></semantics></math> or an <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.2 </span>Task: <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetSentimentOOD" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">SentimentOOD</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetSentimentOOD</annotation></semantics></math></h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the behavior of the estimators over out-of-distribution data, we consider an additional task (<math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetSentimentOOD" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">SentimentOOD</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetSentimentOOD</annotation></semantics></math>) that uses the same models and estimators as <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetSentiment" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Sentiment</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetSentiment</annotation></semantics></math>, but an out-of-distribution evaluation set, <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\testSplit|=4750" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>te</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>4750</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\testSplit|=4750</annotation></semantics></math>. We use the SemEval-2017 Task 4a test set <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, which consists of short-form social media posts that differ in the distribution of topics, language styles, and lengths relative to the movie reviews. We balance the test set, dropping the third class (neutral), setting the semantics of the true labels to be the same as that of the movie reviews: <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\{0={\rm{negative}},1={\rm{positive}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>negative</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>positive</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\{0={\rm{negative}},1={\rm{positive}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.3 </span>Task: <math id="S5.SS1.SSS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetFactcheck" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Factcheck</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetFactcheck</annotation></semantics></math></h4>

<section id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Task.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As a more challenging binary classification task for LLMs, we consider the fact check data of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. The training and calibration sets, a combined total of 6k instances, consist of single sentence statements that have been semi-automatically generated via templates and a knowledge base. The task is to determine whether the statement is true or false, <math id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\{0={\rm{false}},1={\rm{true}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>false</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>true</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\{0={\rm{false}},1={\rm{true}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The held-out eval set, <math id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\testSplit|=245" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>te</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>245</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\testSplit|=245</annotation></semantics></math>, the focus of our analysis, has been constructed by having an LLM generate a statement continued from a true statement not otherwise in the dataset. These evaluation statements are checked manually and assigned labels by human annotators. In addition to being a relatively challenging task that evaluates—at least in principle—the latent knowledge stored within an LLM’s parameters, the test set is representative of the types of distribution shifts over high-dimensional inputs that can be problematic for real applications, and challenging to characterize without model assistance and ground-truth labels. It was observed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> that the accuracy of existing LLM classifiers is dramatically lower on this generated, held-out test set compared to the calibration set. However, these test sentences would seem to also be simple true-false statements, reflecting that it is not always immediately obvious for a human user to detect distribution shifts over high-dimensional inputs. As such, we seek for our models and estimators to reflect such shifts via the predictive uncertainty, as we will not, in general, have true labels at test-time.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Models and Estimators.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reflecting the more challenging task, our base <math id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underlyingNetwork" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>network</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underlyingNetwork</annotation></semantics></math> is the larger 3.2 billion parameter <span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Fast I</span> model from <span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Reexpress one</span>, which is a late fusion of the encoder and decoder of Flan-T5 xl and mT0-base. We additionally compose the <span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Fast I</span> model with <span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Mixtral 8x7B Instruct v0.1</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>. This is achieved by constructing a simple re-ask verification prompt, and then a transform of the final layer of the <span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Mixtral</span> model and the output logits is concatenated to the mean of the hidden states across the input tokens of <span id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Fast I</span>.<span id="footnote19" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">19</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">19</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">19</span>
                  
                  
                  
                This process is the same as that described in the publicly available <span id="footnote19.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Reexpress one</span> tutorial, but as with the <math id="footnote19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetSentiment" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Sentiment</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetSentiment</annotation></semantics></math> task, we discard the adaptors of the on-device application: <a href="https://github.com/ReexpressAI/Example_Data/tree/main/tutorials/tutorial7_factcheck" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/ReexpressAI/Example_Data/tree/main/tutorials/tutorial7_factcheck</a>.</span></span></span> We use the label <math id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\modelFastIMixtralCNNAdaptor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">FastI+Mixtral+adaptor</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\modelFastIMixtralCNNAdaptor</annotation></semantics></math> for a standard exemplar adaptor over the resulting <math id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{h}}\in\mathbb{R}^{5854}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>5854</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{h}}\in\mathbb{R}^{5854}</annotation></semantics></math> trained with a cross-entropy loss, and the label <math id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\modelFastIMixtralSDM" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">FastI+Mixtral+sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\modelFastIMixtralSDM</annotation></semantics></math> for the <math id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation layer over <math id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{h}}\in\mathbb{R}^{5854}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>5854</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{h}}\in\mathbb{R}^{5854}</annotation></semantics></math>. The estimators are otherwise the same as those used for the <math id="S5.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetSentiment" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Sentiment</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetSentiment</annotation></semantics></math> task.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Experiments: Black-box LLM APIs</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we examine the behavior of the estimators when we only have access to a black-box API for an LLM that provides the generated text and the top-1 output log probabilites. In this context with a state-of-the-art model, we examine an additional class of estimators, those that make use of uncertainty estimates explicitly encoded in the surface-level output vocabulary symbols. As a fully held-out test—and real-world use example—we also consider a data quality experiment in which we seek to uncover annotation errors in an existing carefully curated benchmark dataset.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.2.1 </span>Task: Question Answering</h4>

<section id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Task.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our evaluation is over the 4-choice question answering benchmark dataset <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetMMLU" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">MMLU</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetMMLU</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> and a 4-choice subset of the more challenging <span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">MMLU-Pro</span> dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite><span id="footnote20" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">20</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">20</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">20</span>
                  
                  
                  
                Both datasets are available via <a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://huggingface.co/datasets</a></span></span></span>, for which we use the label <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetMMLUPro 4qa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">MMLU-Pro-4qa</mtext><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetMMLUPro 4qa</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\trainSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>tr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\trainSplit</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math> are constructed from 102k instances from the <span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">auxiliary_train</span>, <span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">dev</span>, and <span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">val</span> splits of <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetMMLU" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">MMLU</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetMMLU</annotation></semantics></math> and the MMLU-Pro validation set, the 4-choice subset of which only consists of 29 instances. For <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetMMLU" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">MMLU</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetMMLU</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\testSplit|=14042" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>te</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>14042</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\testSplit|=14042</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetMMLUPro 4qa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">MMLU-Pro-4qa</mtext><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetMMLUPro 4qa</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\testSplit|=5413" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>te</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>5413</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\testSplit|=5413</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Models and Estimators.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use <span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">gpt-4o-2024-08-06</span> (<span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">gpt-4o</span>) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> via the Microsoft Azure service<span id="footnote21" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">21</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">21</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">21</span>
                  
                  
                  
                <a href="https://azure.microsoft.com/en-us/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://azure.microsoft.com/en-us/</a></span></span></span> as the black-box LLM. Given the zero-shot question, the LLM is tasked with providing a structured response against the JSON Schema in Listing <a href="#LST1" title="Listing 1 ‣ A.1 Black-box LLM APIs ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, and the top-1 log probability for each output token. The JSON is parsed for the answer letter, the surface-level symbol of which is the prediction for the <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\estimatorNoReject" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">no-reject</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\estimatorNoReject</annotation></semantics></math> estimator of <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\modelGPTFourO" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">gpt-4o</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\modelGPTFourO</annotation></semantics></math>. We consider the output probability for the answer letter, restricted to those estimates <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq\alpha^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq\alpha^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, as <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\estimatorLetterProb" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">answerStringProb</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\estimatorLetterProb</annotation></semantics></math>. The output JSON is also parsed for the model’s real-valued verbalized uncertainty estimate, which when restricted to estimates <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq\alpha^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq\alpha^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, is the estimator <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\estimatorVerbProb" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">verbalizedProb</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\estimatorVerbProb</annotation></semantics></math>.<span id="footnote22" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">22</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">22</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">22</span>
                  
                  
                  
                An additional class of estimators for black-box LLMs are those that require multiple test-time forward passes through the model, which are related to the Bayesian approaches of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, inter alia]</cite>. We do not consider this class of approaches given the computational costs required of the estimators.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As a final field, the output JSON also contains a short explanation for the response. We take the mean of the output probabilities corresponding to each value of the output JSON and concatenate those three values with a soft feature vector of length 4, where the activated index is that of the surface-level answer choice, for which we use <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\estimatorVerbProb" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">verbalizedProb</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\estimatorVerbProb</annotation></semantics></math> as the value, and all other indexes are 0. This length 7 vector than serves as <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{h}}\in\mathbb{R}^{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒉</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>7</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{h}}\in\mathbb{R}^{7}</annotation></semantics></math> as input to an <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation layer with <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=1000</annotation></semantics></math>. For the resulting <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\modelGPTFourOSDM" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">gpt-4o+sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\modelGPTFourOSDM</annotation></semantics></math> model, we consider the <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\estimatorNoReject" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">no-reject</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\estimatorNoReject</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pLower" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>lower</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pLower</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pCentroid" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>centroid</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pCentroid</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pUpper" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>upper</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pUpper</annotation></semantics></math> estimators. Additional details appear in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Black-box LLM APIs ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.2.2 </span>Task: Data Quality Analysis</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The MMLU-Pro dataset (<math id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetMMLUPro 4qa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">MMLU-Pro-4qa</mtext><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetMMLUPro 4qa</annotation></semantics></math>) is a follow-up to the original <math id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetMMLU" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">MMLU</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetMMLU</annotation></semantics></math> benchmark designed to have more challenging questions and more reliable answer annotations. In the previously described experiment, we examine whether calibration can be maintained over this implied distribution shift. Separately, we consider here whether our method can uncover additional annotation errors, despite the relatively large amount of resources already spent to refine the dataset by the dataset constructors. MMLU-Pro reportedly underwent multiple rounds of review with experts and annotators, including LLM assistance for targeted error detection. We focus on the Computer Science category given that the questions should have unambiguous, objectively verifiable answers. This data quality test is a natural, fully held-out assessment of our approach compared to existing approaches used in practice, with direct, real-world applications. To do so we will examine the annotations among the set of admitted points sorted by <math id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pLower" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>lower</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pLower</annotation></semantics></math> for which <math id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\neq\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≠</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\neq\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the desired behavior is for these points to reflect the aleatory uncertainty (exogenous to the model and estimator) of label annotation errors.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Experiments: Verified Generation</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Next, given the context of the above experiments, we examine the behavior of the <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> network.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Task.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We construct the verification task from the <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetSentiment" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Sentiment</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetSentiment</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetSentimentOOD" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">SentimentOOD</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetSentimentOOD</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetFactcheck" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Factcheck</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetFactcheck</annotation></semantics></math> data described above (§ <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Experiments: Classification ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>), taking the <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> labels of those earlier tasks as the <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\yTask" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>task</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\yTask</annotation></semantics></math> labels. The <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\yVerification" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>verification</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\yVerification</annotation></semantics></math> (or simply <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>) labels (and associated instances) are constructed by synthetically inverting the text of the associated completions, as illustrated in Table <a href="#A1.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ A.2 Verified Generation ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. By design, under the assumption that it is a more challenging learning setting, we do not pair the completions. For example, given a single movie review, it will appear once as part of a user prompt and either the label <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\yVerification=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>verification</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\yVerification=0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\yVerification=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>verification</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\yVerification=1</annotation></semantics></math>, but not both.<span id="footnote23" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">23</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">23</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">23</span>
                
                
                
              As noted in § <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 From sdm Calibration to sdm Networks ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, <math id="footnote23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=0</annotation></semantics></math> instances (here, the constructed negatives) do not participate in next-token fine-tuning, but they are used for training the <math id="footnote23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdmVerificationLayer" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">verificationLayer</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdmVerificationLayer</annotation></semantics></math> and the support set to determine <math id="footnote23.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="footnote23.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For analysis, we then have a standard binary classification task over the force-decoded output, <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="({\bm{x}},\yVerification\in\{0,1\})\in\testSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>verification</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>te</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{x}},\yVerification\in\{0,1\})\in\testSplit</annotation></semantics></math>. We use the following labels for the corresponding datasets: <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetSentimentVerification" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">SentimentVerification</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetSentimentVerification</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\testSplit|=1583" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>te</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1583</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\testSplit|=1583</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetSentimentOODVerification" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">SentimentOODVerification</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetSentimentOODVerification</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\testSplit|=4750" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>te</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>4750</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\testSplit|=4750</annotation></semantics></math>; and <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetFactcheckVerification" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">FactcheckVerification</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetFactcheckVerification</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\testSplit|=245" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>te</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>245</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\testSplit|=245</annotation></semantics></math>. These test sets are useful for analyzing the behavior of the <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdmVerificationLayer" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">verificationLayer</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdmVerificationLayer</annotation></semantics></math>, but they do not reflect a real test-time scenario.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For final evaluation, we take the original test sets from <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetSentiment" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Sentiment</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetSentiment</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetSentimentOOD" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">SentimentOOD</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetSentimentOOD</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\datasetFactcheck" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Factcheck</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\datasetFactcheck</annotation></semantics></math> (§ <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Experiments: Classification ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>) and evaluate the output of the generated JSON for the underlying task labels, <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\yTask" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>task</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\yTask</annotation></semantics></math>, as in a standard evaluation of LLM output.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The corresponding system and user prompts appear in Listing <a href="#LST2" title="Listing 2 ‣ A.2 Verified Generation ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. These design decisions enable examining the instruction-following setting across multiple underlying tasks while enabling reliable evaluation of verification, since there is no ambiguity (up to annotation errors in the original tasks) in <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\yTask" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>task</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\yTask</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\yVerification" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>verification</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\yVerification</annotation></semantics></math>, and we can readily parse the JSON output for the task predictions.<span id="footnote24" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">24</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">24</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">24</span>
                
                
                
              Ill-formatted JSON output is treated as a wrong prediction.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Models and Estimators.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>ref</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> we use <span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Phi-3.5-mini-instruct</span> model (<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\modelPhiThreeFiveInstruct" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">phi3.5</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\modelPhiThreeFiveInstruct</annotation></semantics></math>) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, a 3.8 billion-parameter decoder-only Transformer model, via MLX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, version 0.21.1. To keep the experiments manageable at a level of compute that can be readily replicated on consumer hardware, while still being instructive for future larger-scale experiments, we only update the final linear-layer of <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{pos}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>pos</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{pos}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}_{\rm{pos}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>pos</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}_{\rm{pos}}</annotation></semantics></math>, in the next-token loss (Eq. <a href="#S4.E32" title='In "sdm" Network Next-token Loss. ‣ 4.3.3 "sdm" network ‣ 4.3 From sdm Calibration to sdm Networks ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">32</span></a>); however, we update the full weight matrix of <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}_{\rm{pos}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>pos</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}_{\rm{pos}}</annotation></semantics></math> and not a lower-rank adaptor over these weights. This is instructive in this context, since our data is relatively small, but with <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathcal{V}}|=32064" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>32064</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathcal{V}}|=32064</annotation></semantics></math> and the <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\modelPhiThreeFiveInstruct" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">phi3.5</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\modelPhiThreeFiveInstruct</annotation></semantics></math> hidden dimension of <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3072" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3072</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3072</annotation></semantics></math>, the 100 million parameters of <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}_{\rm{pos}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>pos</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}_{\rm{pos}}</annotation></semantics></math> would be assumed to quickly overfit, leading to degenerate output. Because we only update <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}_{\rm{pos}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>pos</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}_{\rm{pos}}</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\rm{pos}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>pos</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\rm{pos}}</annotation></semantics></math> stays fixed, we only need to train the <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdmVerificationLayer" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">verificationLayer</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdmVerificationLayer</annotation></semantics></math> once before the next-token training loop begins (i.e., Line <a href="#alg4.l12" title='In Algorithm 4 ‣ "sdm" Network Training: "verificationLayer" + Next-token Loop. ‣ 4.3.3 "sdm" network ‣ 4.3 From sdm Calibration to sdm Networks ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> in Alg. <a href="#alg4" title='Algorithm 4 ‣ "sdm" Network Training: "verificationLayer" + Next-token Loop. ‣ 4.3.3 "sdm" network ‣ 4.3 From sdm Calibration to sdm Networks ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> is not needed), and we exclude the weights of <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{neg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>neg</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{neg}}</annotation></semantics></math> as input to the <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation layer, since they are identical to those of <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{pos}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>pos</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{\rm{pos}}</annotation></semantics></math>. As such, the input to the <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm</annotation></semantics></math> activation of the <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdmVerificationLayer" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">verificationLayer</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdmVerificationLayer</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left({\rm{mean}}({\bm{h}}_{\rm{pos}}),{\bm{h}}^{-1}_{\rm{pos}}\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{2\cdot 3072}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>mean</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>pos</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>pos</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>3072</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left({\rm{mean}}({\bm{h}}_{\rm{pos}}),{\bm{h}}^{-1}_{\rm{pos}}\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{2\cdot 3072}</annotation></semantics></math>, the concatenation of the average of the final hidden states (across tokens) with the final hidden state that predicts the end of sequence delimiter (here, the final closing bracket in the JSON output).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this setting, our primary comparison is against the full model before fine-tuning, for which we use the label <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\modelPhiThreeFiveInstructSDM" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">phi3.5+sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\modelPhiThreeFiveInstructSDM</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, only the <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdmVerificationLayer" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">verificationLayer</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdmVerificationLayer</annotation></semantics></math> layer is trained, here for <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J=10</annotation></semantics></math> iterations of 50 epochs, but the evaluation is still over completions generated via greedy decoding (i.e., <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatornamewithlimits{arg\,max}</annotation></semantics></math>) over <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdm({\bm{z}}_{\rm{neg}},{\bm{z}}_{\rm{pos}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sdm</mtext><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>neg</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>pos</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdm({\bm{z}}_{\rm{neg}},{\bm{z}}_{\rm{pos}})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\q=e-2,d=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\q=e-2,d=1</annotation></semantics></math>. The fine-tuned model (<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\modelPhiThreeFiveInstructSDMNetwork" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">phi3.5+sdmNetwork</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\modelPhiThreeFiveInstructSDMNetwork</annotation></semantics></math>) uses this same <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sdmVerificationLayer" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">verificationLayer</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sdmVerificationLayer</annotation></semantics></math>, but it is also trained for 5 epochs with <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\rm{min}}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\rm{min}}=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\rm{max}}=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\rm{max}}=0.1</annotation></semantics></math> using the next-token loss of Alg. <a href="#alg4" title='Algorithm 4 ‣ "sdm" Network Training: "verificationLayer" + Next-token Loop. ‣ 4.3.3 "sdm" network ‣ 4.3 From sdm Calibration to sdm Networks ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. We choose the model weights (as in Line <a href="#alg4.l14" title='In Algorithm 4 ‣ "sdm" Network Training: "verificationLayer" + Next-token Loop. ‣ 4.3.3 "sdm" network ‣ 4.3 From sdm Calibration to sdm Networks ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> of Alg. <a href="#alg4" title='Algorithm 4 ‣ "sdm" Network Training: "verificationLayer" + Next-token Loop. ‣ 4.3.3 "sdm" network ‣ 4.3 From sdm Calibration to sdm Networks ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Similarity-Distance-Magnitude Universal Verification' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) as those that maximize the count of admitted points over <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\calibrationSplit" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>ca</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\calibrationSplit</annotation></semantics></math> via <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pLower" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>lower</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pLower</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, further restricted to <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\yTask=\yTaskPrediction" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>task</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>task</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\yTask=\yTaskPrediction</annotation></semantics></math>, which is determined by parsing the generated JSON output. For both models, <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\modelPhiThreeFiveInstructSDM" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">phi3.5+sdm</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\modelPhiThreeFiveInstructSDM</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\modelPhiThreeFiveInstructSDMNetwork" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">phi3.5+sdmNetwork</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\modelPhiThreeFiveInstructSDMNetwork</annotation></semantics></math>, we consider the <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\estimatorNoReject" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">no-reject</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\estimatorNoReject</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pLower" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>lower</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pLower</annotation></semantics></math> estimators.<span id="footnote25" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">25</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">25</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">25</span>
                
                
                
              In this context, <math id="footnote25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pCentroid" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>centroid</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pCentroid</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="footnote25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pUpper" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>upper</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pUpper</annotation></semantics></math> are less meaningful as a comparison since <math id="footnote25.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pLower" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>lower</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pLower</annotation></semantics></math> is used as part of the aforementioned stopping criteria for <math id="footnote25.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\modelPhiThreeFiveInstructSDMNetwork" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">phi3.5+sdmNetwork</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\modelPhiThreeFiveInstructSDMNetwork</annotation></semantics></math>, and are thus excluded.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Results</h2>

<figure id="S6.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Comparison of relevant estimators for the standard document classification setting, <math id="S6.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\prime}=\hbox{\pagecolor{correctPredictionColor}0.95}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mtext style="--ltx-bg-color:#F2FFF2;" mathbackground="#F2FFF2">0.95</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\prime}=\hbox{\pagecolor{correctPredictionColor}0.95}</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="S6.T1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F2FFF2;">N/A</span> indicates all predictions were rejected, which is preferred over falling under the expected accuracy. <math id="S6.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=|\text{Admitted}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mtext>Admitted</mtext><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=|\text{Admitted}|</annotation></semantics></math>, the count of non-rejected documents.</figcaption>
